the degrees of the polynomials involved while increasing the Jacobian. This trade-off is
sometimes very useful s illustrated in Appendix II.
     In this article we make an effort to compare our results and our methods with
the published one ([A.l]). The explicit references are included in various places.
       In Appendix I we collect all needed propositions of the polynomial Solutions of the
differential equations involved. There is a simple Computer program essentially based on
the discussion at the end to determine the possible counterexamples to the Jacobian
conjecture for all polynomials of degrees less than or equal to 100. In fact there are only
very few possibilities. However the number of coefficients of those possible counter-
examples r nge from 3370 to 7328. In Appendix II we give some theoretic arguments
to reduce the number of coefficients to at most 12. Thus it becomes a computable problem
to show that the Jacobian conjecture is valid for all polynomials of degrees less than
or equal to 100. The direct computation is presented in Appendix II.
     The Computer program is a routine one and will be furnished to anyone upon
request. It uses 147.148 sec of a CDC 6500 and costs only $2.41.
      After many years of research we feel little Information about the distribution of
roots can be added to the present article. As we pointed out before, these distribution
of roots correspond to the desingularization at oo. The geometric approach of de-
singularization will probably add only little Information. Our Appendix II shows that the
Jacobian conjecture is quite plausible. Even if it is false a counterexample may require
a large number of coefficients and is thus almost uncheckable. If the Jacobian conjecture
is valid, the logical sequence to the present article is to show that a part of the data
collected thus far is not permissible algebraically. The arguments in Appendix II implicitly
imply that the tree data deduced thus far are permissible for two individual polynomials
f(x, y) and g(x, y). This is a fact. The only hope, in our opinion, is to put all these tree
data in the defining equation of f(x, y) and g(x, y) over k[x] to establish their non-
permissibility. However we have had limited success in this complicated task.
