/private/var/folders/80/jm5p82hn56g0crpvv9xjzrc00000gn/T/jc2-lane.l8KjQg/inputs/d1-subtree-review-grok46-20260902.md:176:- (d) the leading form of `g` at `sigma_1` has `a_1` distinct simple roots.
/private/var/folders/80/jm5p82hn56g0crpvv9xjzrc00000gn/T/jc2-lane.l8KjQg/inputs/d1-subtree-review-grok46-20260902.md:186:The `r=1` clause is a different, terminal conclusion (only `D≠0`, i.e. not a detector at the general point). It does **not** claim that `p(pi)` has simple roots, and it does not produce a disc `D_0`. Def 5.1 is introduced (p.179) under "for some `r>=2` we identify a tower". There is no `M_0`. That is the halt.
/private/var/folders/80/jm5p82hn56g0crpvv9xjzrc00000gn/T/jc2-lane.l8KjQg/inputs/d1-subtree-review-grok46-20260902.md:188:D1-STAR does not contradict this. It supplies a Jacobian reason why a further major step would be empty of `g`-clusters: after pairwise separation the subdiscs are singletons, and a singleton with `V_1=1` cannot satisfy Def 5.1(1) at a nonexistent level 0 (`(m/n)·1=d/e` is not an integer). Formally "major" under Theorem (5) at `r=1` (`#roots<=n/(n+m)<1` never holds for a nonempty disc), unable to extend.
/private/var/folders/80/jm5p82hn56g0crpvv9xjzrc00000gn/T/jc2-lane.l8KjQg/inputs/d1-subtree-review-grok46-20260902.md:192:Prop 4.6 at `r=1` does not already give (d). OPEN[STAR-REALISABILITY] is well-posed: a forced repeated root of the level-1 leading form would contradict D1-STAR and kill the skeleton. Bounded quantity: the multiplicity partition of `e V_2` at `sigma_1`. Leave OPEN.
